{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>בֶּאֱרֹת בֶּאֱרֹת חֵמָר — wells, wells of bitumen.</b> There were many wells there from which people would take earth for building clay. And the midrashic Aggadah says that the clay was kneaded in them and a miracle was done for the king of Sodom so that he emerged from there, because there were some among the nations who did not believe in Abram, that he was saved from the fiery furnace; once this one emerged from the bitumen, they believed in Abram retroactively — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 14:10). And there is no doubt that the meaning of בֶּאֱרֹת חֵמָר is wells in which there was mire and clay, as in the matter that is written (Jeremiah 38:6), \"and in the pit there was no water but only clay, and Jeremiah sank in the clay,\" and it is written (Psalms 40:3), \"and He brought me up out of the pit of tumult, out of the miry clay.\" And it is possible that he emerged from there naturally, without a miracle. But I am astonished by this Aggadic midrash, for the nations who did not believe that HaKadosh Barukh Hu performed a miracle for Abraham — upon seeing the miracle of the king of Sodom they would not increase their faith in HaKadosh Barukh Hu, for the king of Sodom was an idol worshiper; behold, his miracle would either strengthen the hands of idol worshipers, or they would believe that all miracles come about through sorcery or by a remote possibility of chance, and his miracle would introduce doubt into the hearts of those who believe in the miracle of Abraham. Perhaps, however, they interpret \"and the king of Sodom went out to meet him\" — that he emerged from the pit when Abraham passed over it, for a miracle was performed for him for Abraham's honor, that he went out to meet him to honor him and to bless him. And it is possible that Abraham, on his return, looked at that pit, for he wished to rescue the kings and restore their property to them, and behold, the miracle was performed through him. And if a miracle was performed for the king of Sodom for Abraham's honor, how much more so is it reasonable to believe that a miracle would be performed for Abraham to save his life from death. And let us say that the king of Gomorrah had already died when Abraham passed over him, or that he fell into another pit, for \"there\" is a hint to the valley."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The lemma repeats בֶּאֱרֹת (wells) twice, reflecting the full phrase בֶּאֱרֹת בֶּאֱרֹת חֵמָר in the verse; this is rendered literally. HaKadosh Barukh Hu (הקב\"ה) is the standard reverent periphrasis and is rendered as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:29:03.386755251Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}